<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  OmniBridge notification fixture — TEST ONLY.

  Deliberately absent, and the absence is the point:

    INTERNET · ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE · READ/WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE ·
    READ_MEDIA_* · READ_CONTACTS · ACCESS_*_LOCATION · CAMERA ·
    RECORD_AUDIO · QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES · BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE ·
    SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW · FOREGROUND_SERVICE · RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED

  This app can post its own notifications and nothing else. It cannot reach
  the network, so there is nowhere for anything it is told to display to go.

  POST_NOTIFICATIONS is the one permission it holds and the one thing it is
  for. On API 33+ it is a runtime permission; the certification script grants
  it with `pm grant`, exactly as it does for OmniBridge itself.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_dialog_info"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">

        <!--
          One activity, two jobs.

          Launched from the launcher, it shows what it can do and does
          nothing. Launched with an `op` extra, it performs one operation and
          finishes without drawing.

          It is `exported` because `adb shell am start` runs as the shell user
          and cannot reach a component that is not. That is a real surface and
          it is stated rather than hidden: any app on the device can make this
          fixture post a notification with text of its choosing. What it
          cannot do is make it post as anybody else, reach the network, or
          read anything — so the surface is equivalent to the caller posting
          its own notification, which needs no help from here. On
          certification hardware the fixture is installed for the run and
          uninstalled after it.

          The launcher entry is not decoration: OmniBridge's app picker can only
          offer apps a person can open from their home screen, plus apps that
          happen to be notifying right now. Without it this fixture would be
          invisible in the picker until it was already notifying — which is
          precisely the ordering problem `com.android.shell` has.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".FixtureActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:excludeFromRecents="false"
            android:label="@string/app_name">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
